# ---------- self-serve (API-key) server ----------
SS = CFG["self_serve"]


def render_self_serve() -> None:
    url, name, hdr, env = SS["mcp_url"], SS["server_name"], SS["auth_header"], SS["env_var"]
    vscode = {
        "inputs": [{"type": "promptString", "id": "gupshup-api-key", "description": "Gupshup API key", "password": True}],
        "servers": {name: {"type": "http", "url": url, "headers": {hdr: "${input:gupshup-api-key}"}}},
    }
    body = f"""{GENERATED}# Self-serve: Gupshup API MCP

For developers and businesses on the **Gupshup messaging API** ([reference]({SS['api_docs_url']})). This MCP server is hosted on the Gupshup docs portal. Connect it to your AI client and your agent can work with the messaging API: WhatsApp, SMS, RCS and more.

> Enterprise customers (analytics, templates and campaigns through OAuth sign-in) use the [Enterprise server](clients/README.md) instead. You can connect both.

| | |
|---|---|
| Server URL | `{url}` |
| Transport | Streamable HTTP |
| Auth | Your Gupshup **API key** in the `{hdr}` header |
| API reference | {SS['api_docs_url']} |

**Get your API key** from your Gupshup account dashboard. Keep it out of shared or committed files: the snippets below read it from the `{env}` environment variable, or prompt for it.

```bash
export {env}="<your Gupshup API key>"
```

## Claude Code
```bash
claude mcp add --transport http --scope user {name} {url} --header "{hdr}: ${env}"
```

## Cursor
[![Add to Cursor]({CURSOR_BADGE})]({cursor_https(name, url, {hdr: "${env:" + env + "}"})})

The button adds the server with the key read from `{env}`: set that variable, then restart Cursor. Or add it to `~/.cursor/mcp.json` yourself:
```json
{json.dumps({"mcpServers": {name: {"url": url, "headers": {hdr: "${env:" + env + "}"}}}}, indent=2)}
```

## VS Code
`.vscode/mcp.json`. VS Code prompts for the key once and stores it securely:
```json
{json.dumps(vscode, indent=2)}
```

## OpenAI Codex CLI
`~/.codex/config.toml`:
```toml
[mcp_servers.{name}]
url = "{url}"
env_http_headers = {{ "{hdr}" = "{env}" }}
```

## Windsurf / Devin Desktop
```json
{json.dumps({"mcpServers": {name: {"serverUrl": url, "headers": {hdr: "${env:" + env + "}"}}}}, indent=2)}
```

## Claude Desktop and other stdio-only clients
Use the [`mcp-remote`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/mcp-remote) bridge to add the header:
```json
{json.dumps({"mcpServers": {name: {"command": "npx", "args": ["-y", "mcp-remote", url, "--header", f"{hdr}:${{{env}}}"], "env": {env: "<your Gupshup API key>"}}}}, indent=2)}
```

## Claude.ai and ChatGPT (web)
Their custom connectors authenticate with OAuth and don't let you set an API-key header, so this server can't be added there yet. Use one of the clients above.

## Security
- Your API key can send messages and incur charges, so treat it like a password. Don't paste it into chats, issues or committed config files.
- Rotate the key from your Gupshup dashboard if it's exposed.
- Keep your client's tool-approval prompts on for anything that sends messages.

No account yet? Sign up at [gupshup.ai](https://www.gupshup.ai), or talk to [sales]({"mailto:" + CFG['sales_email']}).
"""
    emit("docs/self-serve.md", body)


